# Example .lore.env for the lore-*.sh shell scripts.
#
# Copy this file to $LORE_DIR/.lore.env (default location: the lore data
# directory resolved by lore-common.sh, see LORE_DIR below) and uncomment
# whatever you want to override. Nothing here is required: every variable
# already has a working default, filled in by lore-common.sh (or, for the
# gardener-only network-preflight variables near the end, by
# lore-gardener.sh itself), so an empty or missing .lore.env is a valid
# single-machine setup.
#
# This file is never read directly. lore-common.sh sources it (if present)
# before applying its own defaults, so anything you set here wins. It is
# gitignored by lore.py bootstrap's default .gitignore template, so secrets
# placed here (a telegram bot token, for instance) are never committed.

# Where the lore knowledge base lives. Leave unset to use the default
# resolution order: an already-set $LORE_DIR wins, else the lore/ directory
# next to this skill's own install (<config-dir>/lore), else ~/.claude/lore.
# LORE_DIR="$HOME/.claude/lore"

# Prefix used when building scheduler job labels/unit names (refresh,
# gardener, watchdog): "${LORE_LAUNCHD_LABEL_PREFIX}gardener" for a launchd
# label, matched the same way against "systemctl --user list-timers" and
# "crontab -l" output on Linux. Change this if you run more than one lore
# install on the same machine and need their scheduled jobs to have distinct
# names.
# LORE_LAUNCHD_LABEL_PREFIX="com.lore."

# Config directory passed to the gardener's headless agent run via
# CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. Point this at whichever Claude Code account/config
# should run the nightly maintenance pass.
# LORE_AGENT_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.claude"

# Full command used to launch the gardener's headless agent run, including
# its prompt flag. Defaults to whatever "claude" resolves to on $PATH plus
# "-p" (headless/print mode). Override this to point at a differently named
# binary or a wrapper script; whatever you set here must accept the prompt
# text as the next positional argument, the way "claude -p" does. Split with
# a plain word split (IFS), not a shell, so a value needing an embedded space
# in a single argument cannot be expressed here.
# LORE_GARDENER_CMD="claude -p"

# Extra arguments appended to the gardener invocation, for example to pin a
# model or effort level. Left empty by default so the gardener uses
# whichever model your claude CLI defaults to. Same word-split rule as
# LORE_GARDENER_CMD above.
# LORE_GARDENER_MODEL_ARGS="--model sonnet --effort high"

# Set to 1 to keep ANTHROPIC_API_KEY set in the gardener's environment.
# Default (0) blanks it so the gardener authenticates the same way an
# interactive session would (OAuth), not via API key billing.
# LORE_KEEP_API_KEY=0

# Path to an optional role-marker file. When set, lore-gardener.sh,
# lore-refresh.sh and lore-watchdog.sh all idle instead of running whenever
# this file's content is exactly "secondary"; use this to designate which
# machine runs the scheduled jobs when the same lore.env is shared across
# machines. Empty (the default) disables the check entirely, so a
# single-machine install always runs.
# LORE_ROLE_FILE=""

# Command run before the gardener's main headless-agent launch (I13); a
# non-zero exit is classified "auth" and skips the run without spending the
# night's attempt. Default checks whatever "claude" resolves to on $PATH; a
# custom LORE_GARDENER_CMD wrapper likely needs a matching override here.
# LORE_GARDENER_AUTH_CHECK="claude auth status"

# --- Alerting (I10) --------------------------------------------------------
#
# alert() (lore-common.sh) always appends one line to $LORE_DIR/.alerts.log
# regardless of the settings below; LORE_ALERT_PROVIDER selects what ELSE it
# does with a subject/body pair.

# One of: log (default, the always-on line above and nothing more), desktop
# (a best-effort local notification: AppleScript on macOS, notify-send on
# Linux), telegram (via lore-alert.sh; needs LORE_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN and
# LORE_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID below), or command (runs $LORE_ALERT_CMD below,
# e.g. a relay script that hops through ssh to a host with reliable egress).
# LORE_ALERT_PROVIDER="log"

# Command run as `$LORE_ALERT_CMD "<subject>"` when LORE_ALERT_PROVIDER=
# command, with <body> piped to its stdin (so an arbitrarily long body never
# has to survive as a single argv word or an ssh command line). Word-split
# the same way as LORE_GARDENER_CMD; point this at your own relay script,
# not a single already-quoted command line.
# LORE_ALERT_CMD=""

# Telegram bot token and chat id, read by lore-alert.sh when
# LORE_ALERT_PROVIDER=telegram. Never printed to any log or exposed in `ps`
# output (lore-alert.sh feeds curl its request body over stdin, not argv).
# LORE_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=""
# LORE_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=""

# --- Watched file size (H7) -------------------------------------------------
#
# lore-watchdog.sh alerts once per file per day when a watched path exceeds
# LORE_WATCH_FILE_MAX_BYTES. Useful for catching a log or run-log growing
# unbounded between gardener nights. A path that doesn't exist yet is
# silently skipped, not an error.

# Colon-separated list of paths to watch. Empty (the default) watches
# nothing.
# LORE_WATCH_FILES="$LORE_DIR/.gardener/gardener.log:$LORE_DIR/.refresh.log"

# Size threshold in bytes, applied to every path in LORE_WATCH_FILES.
# LORE_WATCH_FILE_MAX_BYTES=19500

# --- Gardener network preflight (H1a) ---------------------------------------
#
# Read by lore-gardener.sh directly (not lore-common.sh: these three are
# gardener-only, so their defaults live next to the code that uses them),
# but still overridable here since lore-common.sh sources this file before
# lore-gardener.sh's own body runs. Before ever launching the headless
# agent, the gardener retries a plain connectivity check against the URL
# below up to _RETRIES times, _INTERVAL_SEC apart; exhausting every attempt
# is classified "network" (H1) without spending the night's attempt.

# LORE_GARDENER_NETWORK_CHECK_URL="https://api.anthropic.com/"
# LORE_GARDENER_NETWORK_CHECK_RETRIES=6
# LORE_GARDENER_NETWORK_CHECK_INTERVAL_SEC=300

# --- Gardener timing ---------------------------------------------------------

# Hard wall-clock kill for the whole gardener run (Layer 2 self-watchdog),
# in seconds. A quota-failure retry only waits for a stated reset time if
# that time is still reachable inside whatever is left of this deadline.
# LORE_GARDENER_DEADLINE_SEC=10800

# Wall-clock timeout for a single headless-agent attempt (Step 4), in
# seconds. Must be comfortably smaller than LORE_GARDENER_DEADLINE_SEC above
# since a retried run needs to fit a second attempt inside the same deadline.
# LORE_GARDENER_CLAUDE_TIMEOUT_SEC=9600

# Set to 1 to run refresh and the pending check, log what the gardener would
# do, then exit before ever launching the headless agent. Used for testing;
# not typically set in a real install's .lore.env.
# LORE_GARDENER_DRYRUN=0

# How stale .gardener/gardener.log has to be (seconds since its last tick)
# before lore-watchdog.sh alerts (once per day, no kickstart: a missed night
# should alert, not fire a heavy job at a random daytime hour). Default is
# one missed night plus a 2 hour grace window.
# LORE_GARDENER_STALE_SEC=93600

# --- Refresh / watchdog timing -----------------------------------------------

# Hard wall-clock kill for a single lore-refresh.sh run (Layer 2
# self-watchdog), in seconds.
# LORE_REFRESH_DEADLINE_SEC=3600

# How stale .refresh.log has to be (seconds since its last tick) before
# lore-watchdog.sh alerts and kicks a fresh refresh run. Default is two
# missed 6-hour intervals plus a 1 hour grace window.
# LORE_WATCHDOG_STALE_SEC=46800

# --- lore.py (not read by the shell scripts above) ---------------------------

# URL doctor's "fetch" check requests, purely to confirm outbound HTTPS
# works; the response body is discarded. Point this at your own reachable
# URL if example.com is blocked or slow on your network.
# LORE_DOCTOR_URL="https://example.com"

# How often the scheduled refresh job is expected to run, in seconds; doctor
# compares this against .refresh.log's last entry to decide whether the
# "refresh-log" check is a PASS or a WARN. Keep this in sync with whatever
# interval you actually schedule lore-refresh.sh at (6 hours, matching the
# shipped launchd/systemd templates, by default).
# LORE_REFRESH_INTERVAL_SEC=21600
